      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1770610313284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "kind of\nlike zookeeper\nhave info about\nnodes",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "kind of\nlike zookeeper\nhave info about\nnodes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "36yCyOJKOizfumN4I30fs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 22504.14189401697,
      "y": -14637.224038426173,
      "width": 1447.599853515625,
      "height": 2205,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1381117797,
      "version": 3877,
      "versionNonce": 867487178,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1770654000721,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Scalability: \n    - shard the data across multiple node\n\n2. What about reliability and durability ? \n    - replicate the data to other node or \n      other data centre\n    - Approach \n        - replicate data across these node \n        - may be some k nodes. \n        - one will be leader other will be like\n          follower\n        - here we have approach like master-slave , or master-master -> \n          cassandra/dynamodb work on maaster-master\n\n3. what about consistency ? \n    - this is tough problem. We can use quorem for read r, write w, total n\n        - Strong consistent:\n            - read -> last update write alwasy\n        - weak consistent\n            - may not see the latest.\n        - eventual:\n            - may not see the latest, but eventually will have.\n\n        - r+w > n -> consistent.\n        - r = 1, w = n -> read fast write slow.\n        - r = n , w = 1 -> write fast read slow.\n        - r+w <= n -> no 100% consistency guarantee.\n        -> this is done by coordinator nodes ( or data plane ) \n    \n\n4. What about distributed consensus issues.\n    - node got fail \n        - we can maintain the heart beat of each node with \n          gossip protocol.\n        - in case some node is down, each node will be aware of that\n        - and in our definition, of k -> we can assume \n          working k nodes not failed. \n        - \n    - node returning some wrong info, basically node is fine\n      but malfunctioned. ( Byzantine failure ) \n        - this problem is tough to solve. \n        - but afaik still this is expensive to solve and dynamodb , cassandra\n          doesn't solve this, also this is very very rare.\n        - we do have some stand algorithm to solve distrbiteud failures\n        - raft doesn't solve this. \n        - so in this case also we'll use that won't be the case. \n\n\n5. How we are resolving conflict in case of weak or eventually \n   consistency\n    - in case of strong consistency -> you can have value as per voting.\n    - in case of weak its tough, which to believe. \n    - there are multiple ways one is like do LWW, aws dynamodb uses this \n      to upda\n    - and fanout to other nodes for eventual consistency.\n\n    - flow of conflict resolution case in case of concurrency: \n        - cassandra and dynamodb uses lww, ususually have timestampe in nanoseconds. \n        - 5 nodes -> a update -> \"a\" , 2 update \"b\", last one update -> \"a\" due to \n          timestamp, now these update will again paas to other nodes and they will also update\n          on their side.\n   \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Scalability: \n    - shard the data across multiple node\n\n2. What about reliability and durability ? \n    - replicate the data to other node or \n      other data centre\n    - Approach \n        - replicate data across these node \n        - may be some k nodes. \n        - one will be leader other will be like\n          follower\n        - here we have approach like master-slave , or master-master -> \n          cassandra/dynamodb work on maaster-master\n\n3. what about consistency ? \n    - this is tough problem. We can use quorem for read r, write w, total n\n        - Strong consistent:\n            - read -> last update write alwasy\n        - weak consistent\n            - may not see the latest.\n        - eventual:\n            - may not see the latest, but eventually will have.\n\n        - r+w > n -> consistent.\n        - r = 1, w = n -> read fast write slow.\n        - r = n , w = 1 -> write fast read slow.\n        - r+w <= n -> no 100% consistency guarantee.\n        -> this is done by coordinator nodes ( or data plane ) \n    \n\n4. What about distributed consensus issues.\n    - node got fail \n        - we can maintain the heart beat of each node with \n          gossip protocol.\n        - in case some node is down, each node will be aware of that\n        - and in our definition, of k -> we can assume \n          working k nodes not failed. \n        - \n    - node returning some wrong info, basically node is fine\n      but malfunctioned. ( Byzantine failure ) \n        - this problem is tough to solve. \n        - but afaik still this is expensive to solve and dynamodb , cassandra\n          doesn't solve this, also this is very very rare.\n        - we do have some stand algorithm to solve distrbiteud failures\n        - raft doesn't solve this. \n        - so in this case also we'll use that won't be the case. \n\n\n5. How we are resolving conflict in case of weak or eventually \n   consistency\n    - in case of strong consistency -> you can have value as per voting.\n    - in case of weak its tough, which to believe. \n    - there are multiple ways one is like do LWW, aws dynamodb uses this \n      to upda\n    - and fanout to other nodes for eventual consistency.\n\n    - flow of conflict resolution case in case of concurrency: \n        - cassandra and dynamodb uses lww, ususually have timestampe in nanoseconds. \n        - 5 nodes -> a update -> \"a\" , 2 update \"b\", last one update -> \"a\" due to \n          timestamp, now these update will again paas to other nodes and they will also update\n          on their side.\n   \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TWJKMIc48nr6GNEZ2z6aM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20173.049819425753,
      "y": -12578.874385479667,
      "width": 877.7996826171875,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1887688645,
      "version": 1833,
      "versionNonce": 1033622986,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1770623939290,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Read -> \n\n1. Happy Case. (at node level, but multiple nodes can \n    depending upon quorems ) : \n    - check in memtable \n    - if exist return \n    - else, check in sst table from level 0 to level n \n    - each level , we have multiple segments. \n    - each segment have index \n    - and bloom filter\n    - first they check in filter \n    - else check in index \n    - and continue from segment1 - segmentn to that level\n    - and go upto n level. \n    \n\n\nWrite -> \n\n2. Happy Case: \n    - first write in wal \n    - memtable \n    - later on flush to sst table at level l0 \n    - backend compaction\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Read -> \n\n1. Happy Case. (at node level, but multiple nodes can \n    depending upon quorems ) : \n    - check in memtable \n    - if exist return \n    - else, check in sst table from level 0 to level n \n    - each level , we have multiple segments. \n    - each segment have index \n    - and bloom filter\n    - first they check in filter \n    - else check in index \n    - and continue from segment1 - segmentn to that level\n    - and go upto n level. \n    \n\n\nWrite -> \n\n2. Happy Case: \n    - first write in wal \n    - memtable \n    - later on flush to sst table at level l0 \n    - backend compaction\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
